WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:01.140
Hey Crazies.

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:04.340
Once or twice, you might have heard someone say something like:

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:07.340
The fabric of the universe!

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:09.640
There’s not really a fabric though.

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:11.400
It’s just an analogy.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:16.620
It’s meant to make space and time sound more tangible, like something that can be changed.

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:18.860
But what if there was a fabric?

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:20.840
How elastic would it be?

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:28.000
This episode was made possible by generous supporters on Patreon.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:33.120
The interaction between spacetime and matter is governed by Einstein’s equation.

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:36.920
It looks something like this, which can be a bit intimidating.

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:39.180
Ok, it’s a lot intimidating.

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:41.980
But all this stuff is really just one thing:

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:43.680
spacetime curvature.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:47.000
This other thing is the energy in that spacetime.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:49.640
More energy means more curvature.

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:52.340
Less energy means less curvature.

00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:54.280
As John Wheeler once said:

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:59.200
“Spacetime tells matter how to move and matter tells spacetime how to curve.”

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:01.420
What’s that have to do with elasticity though?

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:03.400
More than you might think.

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:06.540
We’ll set this thing aside and come back to it later.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:08.860
Milton has been bugging me for a lately job anyway.

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:10.060
He can hold onto it for us.

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:11.340
[Alien Noises]

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:14.120
Space Aliens. Can’t take them anywhere.

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:17.640
Anyway, let’s talk about elasticity for a minute.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:20.800
We’ll start with the easiest example: a spring.

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:25.620
The tension in a spring is just a constant times how much it stretched.

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:28.620
We call that constant the “spring stiffness”

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:32.900
because the bigger it is, the more force it takes to stretch the spring.

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:39.060
The stiffer the spring, the less elastic it is, so that constant tells us about its elasticity,

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:41.740
which is exactly what we’re interested in.

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:44.260
This relationship is Hooke’s law.

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:47.660
The force is what we’d call an external cause.

00:01:47.660 --> 00:01:52.260
For example, our spring has a weight hanging on it that causes it to stretch.

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:56.080
The stretch itself is what we’d call an internal response.

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:59.440
We’re all familiar with how things respond externally.

00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:02.440
Newton’s second law says they accelerate,

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:05.580
but things also respond internally.

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:10.300
The ball compresses because the back of it accelerates before the front does.

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:13.760
Other causes can even make objects change shape.

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:19.040
An external cause will always result in some kind of internal response.

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:23.180
Generally, we’d write that like this, which is still called Hooke’s law

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:25.300
because that’s not confusing.

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:29.240
The response is proportional to cause.

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:35.680
The point is we have a general rule for elasticity, one that applies to more than just springs.

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:39.360
The formal names for these two measurements are stress and strain.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:41.580
But I like cause and response better.

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:43.460
They’re less jargon.

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:48.280
Just like with springs, this constant measures how rigid the material is.

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:49.960
It’s the rigidity.

00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:51.520
Isn’t that the opposite of what we want?

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:53.420
Hmm, I suppose you’re right.

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:56.760
Let’s take that constant and put it on the other side.

00:02:56.760 --> 00:03:00.960
That way, the equation takes us from the cause to the response,

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:02.680
as it should be.

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:08.000
We’ll call one-over-rigidity “the elasticity” to make our lives easier.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:10.909
I feel like I’ve seen a relationship like this before.

00:03:10.909 --> 00:03:12.300
[Alien Noises]

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:14.860
Right! Right! Einstein’s equation!

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:19.220
Earlier, we saw that spacetime curvature is proportional to energy.

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:23.400
In fact, that energy is the reason spacetime curves,

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:25.260
which makes it the cause.

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:27.840
And the curvature is the response.

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:30.120
It’s the same [BEEP] equation!

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:36.500
That means, if spacetime were some kind of material, this constant would be its elasticity

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:39.060
and we happen to know what that constant is.

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:42.420
It’s on the order of 10-to-the-minus-43.

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:46.180
That’s 43 places after the decimal point,

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:48.580
which is very little elasticity.

00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:51.140
Spacetime is super rigid.

00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:53.640
And that definitely makes sense.

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:58.480
It takes the mass of something like a planet or moon to even notice gravity.

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:03.340
It’s the weakest of the fundamental forces because spacetime is so rigid.

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:05.100
Where did that constant come from though?

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:06.880
What do you mean? I just plugged stuff in and...

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:08.940
No no, I mean, why is it that number?

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:12.980
Oh! Because it has to be consistent with Newtonian gravity.

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:18.620
Every new model we come up with has to be consistent with older time-tested models.

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:21.160
Say we’ve got two rocks floating in space.

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:23.120
Each one of them has a mass.

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:26.380
Maybe one them is bigger than the other, it doesn’t matter.

00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:29.140
Newton says they each experience a force

00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:32.640
related to both of their masses and the distance between them.

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:38.220
Which is great and all, but forces aren’t actually that useful for deeper understanding.

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:40.580
I mean, they make intuitive sense to us.

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:47.260
It’s just we’ve found that writing laws in terms of energy instead makes them look less ugly.

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:48.980
To the timeline!

00:04:48.980 --> 00:04:52.780
Newton’s law of gravity was developed in the 1660s.

00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:55.840
But in 1813, Siméon Poisson

00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:58.940
Siméon P. Siméon. Siméon.

00:04:58.940 --> 00:05:00.440
Siméon Poisson.

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:01.700
Poisson.

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:02.980
Poisson.

00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:04.580
[Laughing] [BEEP]

00:05:04.580 --> 00:05:09.520
Siméon Poisson came up with an equation we now call Poisson’s equation,

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:13.800
which transforms Newton’s force law into an energy law.

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:17.880
Granted, it wasn’t called energy back then, but that’s what it was,

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:19.940
whether it was called that or not.

00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:25.030
Basically, this says a change in energy across space is caused by a mass.

00:05:25.030 --> 00:05:29.420
Actually, that’s not energy. That’s energy per unit mass or potential.

00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:31.960
Also, that’s not mass. It’s mass density.

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:35.100
It doesn’t matter if we’re only looking at this conceptually.

00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:40.080
This is energy adjacent and this is mass adjacent.

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:41.500
It’s close enough!

00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:42.740
Ay Yi Yi.

00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:47.740
If we map out those energy values across space near something like the Earth,

00:05:47.740 --> 00:05:49.140
it looks like this.

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:51.200
It’s a bunch of negative numbers.

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:55.200
The Earth is creating something we call a “gravitational well.”

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:59.420
But, if we’re going to call it a well, we might as well imagine it as one.

00:05:59.420 --> 00:06:02.260
Did you catch that? We might as well.

00:06:02.260 --> 00:06:04.180
Because it’s a gravitational well.

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:06.000
I’m such a dork.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:11.500
Let’s imagine that each of these numbers represents the depth of the well at that location.

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:16.200
In that case, we’d have something that looks like this, which should look familiar.

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:20.520
It’s the one graphic you usually see when someone is talking about curved spacetime.

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:23.880
Except, I just made it without using Einstein’s equation.

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:29.720
It’s made using Poisson’s equation, an equation based on Newtonian gravity.

00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:31.340
Bubbles burst!

00:06:31.340 --> 00:06:36.360
To be fair though, that gravitational well does look kind of like a fabric.

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:40.780
Almost like Poisson’s equation is just one step away from Einstein’s.

00:06:40.780 --> 00:06:44.980
In fact, Einstein’s equation is also an energy equation.

00:06:44.980 --> 00:06:48.620
Every term in here is also energy adjacent.

00:06:48.620 --> 00:06:52.920
So, under the right conditions, it should simplify to Poisson’s equation.

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:54.800
And that’s exactly what happens.

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:59.300
We call it the Weak-Field Limit, in which we make two assumptions:

00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:00.320
One!

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:03.480
The cause of the curvature is an object made of matter

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:07.660
and the particles in that object are moving slow compared to light.

00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:08.720
Two!

00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:11.380
The curvature is very close to zero.

00:07:11.380 --> 00:07:14.600
It’s pretty much just the spacetime from special relativity

00:07:14.600 --> 00:07:18.480
plus a smidge of curvature, just a smidge though.

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:22.540
Both of these assumptions are perfectly reasonable around something like the Earth,

00:07:22.540 --> 00:07:25.580
where we’d expect Newton’s law of gravity to apply.

00:07:25.580 --> 00:07:29.360
In the weak-field limit, Einstein’s equation looks like this,

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:32.980
which is only a constant factor away from Poisson’s equation.

00:07:32.980 --> 00:07:35.180
It’s the same [BEEP] equation,

00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:37.600
at least in the weak-field limit.

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:43.780
Of course, the fabric analogy is just that, an analogy, and all analogies have problems.

00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:48.820
For one, real-life materials don’t always have a proportional response.

00:07:48.820 --> 00:07:53.040
If you stretch them too much, they can permanently deform or even break.

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:56.720
Spacetime’s response is always proportional.

00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:01.660
That might make you think spacetime is simpler, but it’s just complicated in other ways.

00:08:01.660 --> 00:08:06.880
For example, I’ve been displaying it as a two-dimensional surface because that’s easy,

00:08:06.880 --> 00:08:09.700
but that’s a terribly inaccurate visual.

00:08:09.700 --> 00:08:13.580
Spacetime isn’t two-dimensional. It’s four-dimensional.

00:08:13.580 --> 00:08:18.980
Both the cause and the response are represented by four-dimensional tensors,

00:08:18.980 --> 00:08:21.660
each with 10 unique components.

00:08:21.660 --> 00:08:24.760
The weak-field limit only uses one of them.

00:08:24.760 --> 00:08:29.240
Most of the gravity humans experience on Earth isn’t even space curvature.

00:08:29.240 --> 00:08:31.040
It’s time curvature.

00:08:31.040 --> 00:08:33.820
So how elastic is the fabric of the universe?

00:08:33.820 --> 00:08:36.620
Not very elastic at all actually.

00:08:36.620 --> 00:08:40.060
It’s on the order of 10-to-the-minus-43.

00:08:40.060 --> 00:08:45.860
It takes a ridiculous amount of matter or light in one place to even notice gravity.

00:08:45.860 --> 00:08:51.720
Spacetime might not actually be a fabric, but, whatever it is, it’s pretty rigid stuff.

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:54.820
So, how many of your bubbles did I burst today?

00:08:54.820 --> 00:08:56.500
Please share in the comments.

00:08:56.500 --> 00:08:58.660
